nd measurement disturbs. This review develops quantum Darwinism’s resolution, which locates classicality in the environment as a broadcast medium carrying independent records of an observable. The framework is assembled in information-theoretic terms: quantum mutual information between system and environment fragments, redundancy at an information deficit, the Holevo quantity separating accessible from total correlation, spectrum broadcast structure as the record-bearing state, and the quantum Chernoff information fixing the rate of accumulation. A proposition proving that spectrum broadcast structure forces the classical plateau fragment by fragment is given. Three media — spin registers, oscillator baths, and scattered photons — are examined, followed by account of a record’s life cycle: formation, degradation under intra-environmental scrambling and information backflow, and counterexa

